{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{3}=64\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489213 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489213 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68934" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489213 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489213 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $(-3)\\times ((-8)^{2}\\times \\frac{3}{64}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489213 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b607318-28df-51c2-9737-62f37f3092a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489213 #edb/ref 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, consider the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-8)^{2}\\times \\frac{3}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-8)^{2}\\times \\frac{3}{64} & = \\\\ 64\\times \\frac{3}{64} & = \\\\ \\frac{64}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{64} & = \\\\ \\frac{64\\times 3}{1\\times 64} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{64}\\times 3}{1\\times \\cancel{64}} & = \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)\\times ((-8)^{2}\\times \\frac{3}{64}) & = \\\\ (-3)\\times 3 & = \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489218 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489218 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489218 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489218 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $(\\frac{2}{5}\\times {(-5)}^{2})÷({(-2)}^{5}-(-20)).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489218 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50989de1-e6f3-5831-87a9-51e32375a2a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489218 #edb/ref 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we consider the expressions inside each of the parentheses. The first one is.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}\\times {(-5)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}\\times {(-5)}^{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\times 25 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\times \\frac{25}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\times 25}{5\\times 1} & = \\\\ \\frac{50}{5} & = \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression inside the second parentheses is.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-2)}^{5}-(-20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-2)}^{5}-(-20) & = \\\\ (-32)+20 & = \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{2}{5}\\times {(-5)}^{2})÷({(-2)}^{5}-(-20)) & = \\\\ 10÷(-12) & = \\\\ \\frac{10}{(-12)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{10}{12} & = \\\\ -\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489277 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186489026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489277 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489277 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489277 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $3+(-2+7)^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489277 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dff097a9-6d33-544e-9f71-468162e0a004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489277 #edb/ref 1074 "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nWe begin by performing the operations inside the parentheses. So, first we add $(-2)$ to $7$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+(-2+7)^{2} & =3+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute the exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+{5}^{2} & =3+25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the numbers: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+25=28\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489297 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186489348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489297 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "03c2edcd-575d-58ce-886d-f19883074feb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489297 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186489213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489299 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11b7515f-341c-51bb-9088-1fd8218d06f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489299 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186489199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186489327 #edb/ref 1058 #